  "requirement": "The `habeas-refactor` skill MUST tell agents not to edit evidence files by hand\nto follow a move, and that renaming a function or editing moved text is not a\nmove and needs reassessment.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has three obligations for the `habeas-refactor` skill text: (1) tell agents not to edit evidence files by hand to follow a move; (2) tell them that renaming a function is not a move and needs reassessment; (3) tell them that editing moved text is not a move and needs reassessment.\n\nThe single test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_never_hand_edits_evidence_and_a_rename_is_not_a_move]`, is marked {test}. It reads the real bundled `habeas-refactor` `SKILL.md` through `skill_md` and extracts the `## Gotchas` section with `section`. It then calls `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase and panics on any missing phrase. The phrases map to the obligations like this:\n- Obligation 1: \"Never edit evidence files by hand to follow a move\".\n- Obligation 2: \"A rename is not a move. Renaming a function, type, or other item changes its code\" and \"its evidence needs reassessment\".\n- Obligation 3: \"Editing moved code or text in the same step leaves every record that read it stale\".\n\nThe assertions are enforced, not just executed. Each phrase is specific to one obligation, so removing or weakening that guidance would fail the test.\n\nObligation 3 is checked through the skill's \"stale\" wording rather than the literal words \"needs reassessment\". A stale record is one that needs reassessment, and the skill's own text is the only place this guidance exists. I treat that as adequate, not as a gap.\n\nThe skill is the only subject of this requirement, so there are no distinct modes or inputs to cover. I found no marking gaps and no acknowledgments apply.",
